            <ArticleTitle>A retrospective quality improvement study of ketorolac use following spinal fusion in pediatric patients.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>342-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/NOR.0b013e3181edd876</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">There are studies and literature that support the claim that ketorolac use after spinal fusion in the adult population can increase the risk of pseudarthrosis, instrumentation failure, and/or nonunion. There is limited research when using ketorolac in the pediatric population, especially short-term use.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Chart review of 46 pediatric patients who had prior spinal fusions for scoliosis between July 2003 and August 2005. Twenty-five of the patients received ketorolac and 21 did not. The lengths of stay, incidence of curve progression, and/or incidence of nonunion or instrumentation failure were compared in the 2 groups.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">At the 1-year follow-up, 95% of the patients returned and at the 3-year follow-up, 52% of the patients returned and there was no clinical or radiographic evidence of curve progression, nonunion, or instrumentation failure.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="LEVEL OF EVIDENCE" NlmCategory="METHODS">This is a retrospective study looking at results of 2 patient groups. This is a level III study.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
